// Solution — Module 02, Exercise 04: Freeing Memory
//
// Task 1: as shipped the file runs clean. It writes 42, reads it back, then
// scorches the block. Nothing touches the block after that, so nothing is
// wrong — this is the well-behaved version.
//
// Task 2: uncommenting the peek turns it into use-after-free:
//
//   quicksand at line N, column M
//   this ground was scorched at line 12 (garrisoned at line 10)
//
// The message names both moments — where the block was freed and where it was
// allocated. In C you would get neither. You would get whatever bytes happen
// to be at that address now, which is often the old value, which is why this
// bug survives testing and fails in production.
//
// BONUS (double-free): calling scorch(p) twice reports quicksand as well, and
// names the first scorch. See 03-faults/03-double-free for that one.
//
// BONUS (leak): skipping scorch entirely ends the run with
//   spooked: ancient weeds - 1 pine never scorched (4 bytes), garrisoned at line N
// See 03-faults/04-leaks.

p = garrison(4)
p.poke(i32, 42)
print(f"before scorch: {p.peek(i32)}")

scorch(p)
print("after scorch — the block is gone, and p still points at where it was")

// A pine does not become none when you scorch it. It keeps the same address;
// only the ground under it changed. That is precisely why C cannot warn you:
// the pointer looks exactly as valid as it did a line earlier.

// Reading through it now is use-after-free. This ends the program, so it is
// the last statement in the file.
print("")
print("now reading through the scorched pine...")
val = p.peek(i32)
print(f"unreachable: {val}")
